#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115040 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115213 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "348ff9ef-8ffe-57aa-bafa-9d1a197178fc" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Logarithmic Equations Containing a Constant on One Side"}] :question/id #uuid "d833825d-4ac4-556d-95a1-bb328d5b5bb4" :question/math-academy-id "e-4149" :question/problem "If $\\log_2(x^2-6)=\\log_2(10)$, then what is the value of $x$?" :question/worked-solution "Since the logarithms on both sides of the equation have the same base, we can equate the arguments. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\log_{2} (x^{2} - 6) &= \\log_{2} (10) \\\\\nx^{2} - 6 &= 10 \\\\\nx^{2} &= 16 \\\\\nx &= ± 4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now check for extraneous solutions by substituting back into the original equation:\n\n- Substituting $x=4$ back into the original equation, we get\n$\\log_{2} (x^{2} - 6)|= \\log_{2} (4^{2} - 6); = \\log_{2} (16 - 6); = \\log_{2} (10).✓$\nTherefore, $x=4$ is a valid solution.\n- Substituting $x=-4$ back into the original equation, we get\n$\\log_{2} (x^{2} - 6)|= \\log_{2} ((-4)^{2} - 6); = \\log_{2} (16 - 6); = \\log_{2} (10).✓$\nTherefore, $x=-4$ is a valid solution.\n\nSo, we conclude that the solutions are $x = \\pm 4$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115042 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115214 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d03f79a4-06f3-5324-a1ec-687659956384" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Logarithmic Equations Containing Nonlinear Arguments"}] :question/id #uuid "088cff27-4c06-542c-876b-d3d2ed7b0504" :question/math-academy-id "e-4150" :question/problem "Solve the equation $\\ln (2x^2)=\\ln (3x)$." :question/worked-solution "Since the logarithms on both sides of the equation have the same base, we can equate the arguments. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\ln (2x^{2}) &= \\ln (3x) \\\\\n2x^{2} &= 3x \\\\\n2x^{2} - 3x &= 0 \\\\\nx(2x - 3) &= 0 \\\\\nx &= 0, \\frac{3}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now check for extraneous solutions by substituting back into the original equation:\n\n- Substituting $x=0$ back into the original equation, we get\n$\\ln (2x^{2})|=^(?)\\ln (3x); \\ln (2(0)^{2})|=^(?)\\ln (3(0)); \\ln (0)|=^(?)\\ln (0)\\cdot$\nwhich is not valid because $\\ln \\left(0\\right)$ is undefined. Therefore, $x=0$ is not a valid solution.\n- Substituting $x=\\dfrac{3}{2}$ back into the original equation, we get\n$\\ln (2x^{2})|=^(?)\\ln (3x); \\ln (2(\\frac{3}{2})^{2})|=^(?)\\ln (3(\\frac{3}{2})); \\ln (\\frac{9}{2})|= \\ln (\\frac{9}{2}).✓$\nTherefore, $x=\\dfrac{3}{2}$ is a valid solution.\n\nTherefore, the only solution is\n\n$$\nx =\\dfrac{3}{2}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115043 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115215 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9025fa2d-5f74-5187-a225-234da9b9810b" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Logarithmic Equations Containing Linear Arguments on Both Sides"}] :question/id #uuid "d164cff6-3d50-57c8-a118-8681aaf0c1f8" :question/math-academy-id "e-4151" :question/problem "Solve $\\log (x-1)=\\log (2x+1)$ for $x$." :question/worked-solution "Since the logarithms on both sides of the equation have the same base, we can equate the arguments. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\log (x - 1) &= \\log (2x + 1) \\\\\nx - 1 &= 2x + 1 \\\\\nx &= -2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now check for extraneous solutions by substituting back into the original equation:\n\n- Substituting $x=-2$ back into the original equation, we get\n$\\log (x - 1)|=^(?)\\log (2x + 1); \\log (-2 - 1)|=^(?)\\log (2(-2) + 1); \\log (-3)|=^(?)\\log (-3)$\nHowever, $\\log (-3)$ is not a real number.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the equation has no real solutions."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233039 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115213 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "348ff9ef-8ffe-57aa-bafa-9d1a197178fc" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Logarithmic Equations Containing a Constant on One Side"}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "58031be4-0ee8-501f-8ac5-6ad86baa2337" :question/math-academy-id "q-100706" :question/problem "Solve the equation $\\operatorname{log}(x-2)=\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{log}(256).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we use the power rule to simplify the right-hand side. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{log}(x-2) & =\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{log}(256) \\\\ \\operatorname{log}(x-2) & =\\operatorname{log}({256}^{1/4}) \\\\ \\operatorname{log}(x-2) & =\\operatorname{log}(4).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the logarithms on both sides of the equation have the same base, we can equate the arguments. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{log}(x-2) & =\\operatorname{log}(4) \\\\ x-2 & =4 \\\\ x & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now check for extraneous solutions by substituting back into the original equation: \n\n- Substituting $x=6$ back into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{log}(x-2) & =\\operatorname{log}(6-2) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{log}(4) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{log}({256}^{1/4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{log}(256).\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $x=6$ is a valid solution."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233699 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115215 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9025fa2d-5f74-5187-a225-234da9b9810b" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Logarithmic Equations Containing Linear Arguments on Both Sides"}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "97031323-cb39-5f57-8186-f731d54414db" :question/math-academy-id "q-26532" :question/problem "Given that ${log}_{2}(3x+1)={log}_{2}(5-x),$ what is the value of $x?$" :question/worked-solution "Since the logarithms on both sides of the equation have the same base, we can equate the arguments. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{2}(3x+1) & ={log}_{2}(5-x) \\\\ 3x+1 & =5-x \\\\ 4x & =4 \\\\ x & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now check for extraneous solutions by substituting back into the original equation: \n\n- Substituting $x=1$ back into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{2}(3(1)+1) & \\overset{?}{=}{log}_{2}(5-1) \\\\ {log}_{2}(4) & ={log}_{2}(4)\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $x=1$ is a valid solution."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115640 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115215 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9025fa2d-5f74-5187-a225-234da9b9810b" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Logarithmic Equations Containing Linear Arguments on Both Sides"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ace4c8e3-fb3b-5341-aa6e-2abec66da4b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186112807} {:answer/id #uuid "e1f33d1e-7bd9-53fe-a25c-210684a86ce2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186112808} {:answer/id #uuid "0b2db6ea-6620-5cbb-a2d3-0f5a6ffe63fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186112809} {:answer/id #uuid "1e1bf617-7333-54ea-9b16-627401a99811" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186112810} {:answer/id #uuid "2d9d835b-b38e-5aee-97b2-4478bdfc12c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No real solutions" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186112811}] :answer-field/id #uuid "731593c9-3830-55ca-8d4d-43433e4c3f4d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186112806}] :question/id #uuid "fb4b4f34-a075-5d22-9a44-1bdf761fc15b" :question/math-academy-id "q-27598" :question/problem "Given that $\\log (3x - 2) = \\log (8 - 2x)$, what is the value of $x$?"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 1